module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AffineCoordinateStrictLocalData

/-! # Polynomial models of the strict-local closed fibre

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 04HX, 05WS and 04GU.
(blueprint: lem-trf-affine-coordinate-strict-local-fibre-comparison).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
universe u
open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits
namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.AffineCoordinateStrictLocal

/-- The two pointed polynomial strict henselizations model the ambient closed fibre and
its selected-coordinate quotient, with their coefficient, coordinate and residue maps.
Source: Stacks Project, Etale Cohomology, Tag 04HX; Algebra, Tags 05WS and 04GU.
(blueprint: lem-trf-affine-coordinate-strict-local-fibre-comparison).

Proof: Write A for the base strict local ring, B for the ambient one and kappa for A/m_A.
The canonical geometric evaluation on A has kernel m_A, so it factors through an
embedding kappa into Omega. Evaluate P = kappa[T_i] at this embedding and at the
geometric evaluations of the ambient coordinates. The hypothesis says that the ideal
L generated by the selected variables lies in the kernel q. Descend this evaluation
to P/L; its prime kernel is the image q/L, by the quotient correspondence for ideals.

Base change the coordinate chart V to Spec A and then to Spec kappa. The affine-space
pullback universal property and AffineSpace.SpecIso identify this closed fibre with
Spec P, preserving coefficients and each coordinate. Apply
lem-trf-smooth-strict-local-closed-fibre-model to the smooth affine-space projection:
it identifies B/(m_A B) with the geometric strict local ring of this Spec P at the
evaluation point, and identifies the map from B with restriction to that fibre.
The affine strict-local comparison (Tag 04HX) identifies this geometric ring with the
pointed strict henselization of P_q. Choose that defining pointed-etale colimit as H0;
its universal cocone supplies presentation0. The structural and evaluation equations
of the two comparisons give coefficient0, coordinate0 and evaluation0.

Now apply the closed-immersion Cartesian comparison (Tag 05WS) to Spec(P/L) in Spec P.
The extended ideal L in H0 is generated by the classes of the selected ambient
coordinates. Its inverse image in B is m_A B + J: the quotient map is surjective, so
lifting the coefficients of a finite sum of multiples gives precisely this inverse
image. The quotient correspondence therefore identifies H0/LH0 with B/(m_A B + J).
Apply the affine strict-local comparison on Spec(P/L), using its quotient evaluation
point and the relative separable residue closure inside the same Omega. This gives H1,
presentation1 and iso1, and preserves every coefficient, surviving variable and residue
evaluation. The kernel prime of each evaluation is prime because Omega is a field;
the canonical residue-field embeddings of the prime localizations give residue0_eq
and residue1_eq. Their restrictions to the relative separable closures give the two
closure algebras and scalar towers.

Conjugate the canonical strict-local map of Spec(P/L) to Spec P by the two affine
comparisons. It is local and sends each polynomial to its quotient class. The maps
from B factor through the successive quotient maps above, giving comparison_quotient;
the same pointedness equations give comparison_residue. Finally any other local map
with the displayed polynomial equation agrees on P_q, since elements outside q are
units. With the displayed residue equation it has the same map of the specified
residue closures inside Omega. Uniqueness for pointed strict henselizations (Tag 04GU)
therefore identifies it with this comparison. No prime is assumed maximal, and all
quotients retain nilpotents. -/
theorem exists_fibre_models {S V : Scheme.{u}} (r : ℕ)
    (e : V ≅ AffineSpace (Index.{u} r) S)
    {Ω : Type u} [Field Ω] [IsAlgClosed Ω] (v : Spec (.of Ω) ⟶ V)
    [IsLocalRing (Base r e v)] (K : Set (Index.{u} r))
    (hK : ∀ i ∈ K, strictHenselizationPointEvaluation v (coordinate r e v i) = 0) :
    Nonempty (FibreModels (Base r e v) (strictHenselizationRing v) (PolynomialRing r e v)
      (Index.{u} r) K (fibreIdeal r e v) (coordinateIdeal r e v K) (polynomialIdeal r e v K)
      (baseMap r e v) ((MvPolynomial.C).comp (IsLocalRing.residue (Base r e v)))
      (coordinate r e v) MvPolynomial.X
      (strictHenselizationPointEvaluation (v ≫ projection r e))
      (strictHenselizationPointEvaluation v)) := by sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.AffineCoordinateStrictLocal
